## Verdict

Passed. `studio/briefs/W-110.md` revision 5 answers the builder's two rulings
and corrects one of its three FYIs that revision 4 had wrong. Eight
behaviours; no genuine-red row touched, so the phase-1 reds already recorded
on `opus/W-110` stand.

## Ruling 1 — behaviour 8(f): the builder's reading is confirmed, and the
## mechanism is named

The row was false as literally written and the builder is right to have
noticed. `apps/web/tests-serve/harness.ts:49-50` carries "node --import tsx,
never npx tsx" in its doc comment, so "its source contains no `npx`" can never
pass. The property wanted is that **no code path invokes `npx`**.

Confirmed with one correction to the method: do not hand-roll a comment
stripper. The repo already exports one — `lex` from
`packages/cli/src/rules/tests.ts`, written for the `test.sleep` rule and
exported for its own test — and L-043 ("containment-helper-duplicated") is the
lesson against writing a second. Measured: `lex(harness)` reports **zero** code
lines matching `/\bnpx\b/` and zero `//` comment lines matching, because the one
occurrence lives in a `/** */` span that `lex` blanks out of `.code` entirely.
So the assertion reads 0 today and reads nonzero the moment a spawn uses `npx`.
8(f) now says exactly that.

## Ruling 2 — `runServedSpy`'s `deepEqual` is in scope, with this shape

In scope. It is the same class as every other edit in *Files owned* — an
existing assertion that pins precisely what this opus changes — and revision 4
under-listed `opus-model.test.ts` by naming only the two served helpers.
Behaviour 8(e) is the row that forces it.

I read the spy (`:1431-1500`) before ruling, because the shape is not simply
"drop the build line": `SPY_FAIL_AT` counts `npm` invocations, so dropping one
invocation moves two of the three cases.

- `servedSuccess` → `{ status: 0, calls: ["--workspace @bisellium/web run test:serve"] }`.
- the `failAt: 1` case, **renamed `servedSuiteFailure`** (there is no build to
  fail) → `{ status: 7, calls: ["--workspace @bisellium/web run test:serve"] }`.
  The spy's first call is now `test:serve`, so this is where a nonzero from the
  suite propagates.
- the `failAt: 2` case is **deleted**. There is no second `npm` call for it to
  fail at, so it would assert nothing the success case does not already assert.
  Deleting a case that has become vacuous is better than keeping a green row
  that proves nothing.
- one hardening while in there: the spy spreads `process.env` wholesale, so
  these rows would inherit `BISELLIUM_OPUS`/`BISELLIUM_STUDIO_DIR` if the suite
  ever ran under a `verify`. The child env clears both, so the rows take the
  script's no-evidence branch deterministically.

Nothing else in that assertion moves: `exitCode`, `unchanged`, `state`,
`e2eRule`, `reviewWithoutServedRefused` and `staleServedBindingRefused` are
untouched.

## The three FYIs

Two taken as reported. Behaviour 3 seeding before the first load is equivalent
— every route rebuilds its snapshot per request (`store.ts` `snap()`), so there
is nothing to invalidate. 7(d)'s symlinked-parent case landing on the step-1
manifest refusal for two findings is right and is what the stated convention
predicts.

The third I checked and the builder is right where **revision 4 was wrong**, so
it is not merely an FYI: 5(g) cannot import `redact` from `@bisellium/shim`.
Measured under bare `node` v25.9.0 — `scripts/served-e2e.test.mjs` runs without
tsx — the package specifier answers `ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND` (the barrel's ESM
`.js` specifiers do not exist on disk) while `packages/shim/src/redact.ts` by
path loads through Node 25's own type stripping. The brief said the wrong one.
This is worth more than a wording fix: `ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND` in a red log is a
module-load failure, which `packages/cli/src/rules/evidence.ts:154-158` reports
as `opus.red_content` — so the brief as written would have cost the builder a
finding on its own red. Corrected, with the reason recorded in the row.

